  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Moldova&#x2019;s National Capacities to Ensure Quality Protection, Education, Health Services and Socio-Economic Opportunities for Ukrainian Refugees</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The conflict in Ukraine has created a regional humanitarian crisis. Over 8.02 million Ukrainians are internally displaced, and 5.98 million have fled into neighbouring countries. Moldova is a top refugee hosting and transit country. Over 460,000 refugees and TCNs have entered Moldova, with many continuing to Romania, Poland and other countries. While the majority of those arriving in neighbouring countries are Ukrainian nationals, Third Country Nationals (TCNs) are also represented among the evacuees. As of mid-May, there are approximately 86,000 refugees and TCNs on Moldovan territory, housed among relatives, in refugee centres and other temporary accommodation. Around 90% of the people arriving in Moldova are women and children. As such, in this project IOM and UNICEF will strengthen national capacities and services to ensure that Ukrainian refugees and TCNs have access to quality protection, education, and health services, and greater opportunities for socio-economic inclusion. The project aims to achieve the following results:  (Protection) Government authorities provide reinforced protection support to Ukrainian refugees and TCNs; (Health) Ukrainian refugees and TCNs have better access to medical services; (Livelihoods and social inclusion) Ukrainian refugees, TCNs and host communities have increased socio-economic opportunities; (Education) Children have improved learning outcomes in line with the OECD benchmarks; (Social services) Protection and well-being of children and their families ensured through a strengthened child protection and social services systems.</narrative>
  </description>
